WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.200
Why is every access
decision a policy decision?

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.838
Because federal Zero Trust policy says
so, and the platform works that way.

3
00:00:07.838 --> 00:00:14.463
M-22-09 requires every request to be evaluated,
with no trust from network location.

4
00:00:14.463 --> 00:00:19.520
NIST describes a policy decision point,
separate from where access is enforced,

5
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.125
and in Entra ID that's Conditional Access.

6
00:00:22.125 --> 00:00:26.679
Its rules are simple: every policy
that applies must be satisfied,

7
00:00:26.679 --> 00:00:30.137
a block wins, and exclusions
override everything.

8
00:00:30.137 --> 00:00:34.062
What it replaces is access decided
once and trusted afterward.

9
00:00:34.062 --> 00:00:36.262
Inside the network, so trusted.

10
00:00:36.262 --> 00:00:38.901
In the group, so allowed, forever.

11
00:00:38.901 --> 00:00:41.484
Those assumptions survive
a migration unless

12
00:00:41.484 --> 00:00:44.200
someone deliberately
replaces them with policy.

13
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.488
Two consequences run through the book.

14
00:00:46.488 --> 00:00:51.500
Every exclusion is a standing hole, so
each one needs an owner and a review.

15
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.128
And policy is only as good
as the attributes it reads,

16
00:00:55.128 --> 00:00:58.320
which is why HR-Driven
Identity matters here.
